  "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1)={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1={tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta +1)={tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta .\n$$",
